/**
\page peano_amr_tex AMR

Adaptive mesh refinement {#section:amr}
========================

 realises a multi-step refinement and erasing[^1] strategy.

1.  Each subpartition asks the action set (clone) for the refinement and
    coarsening decisions at the startup of the mesh traversal. For this,
    they query `getGridControlEvents()`.

2.  The mesh traversal uses this information to trigger erases or
    refinements.

3.  In the subsequent mesh traversal, the mesh is actually altered.

or-based refinement and refinement arguments
--------------------------------------------

In , a cell is refined, if one of its vertices carries a refinement
flag. That is, we flag vertices (not cells), and the cells are refined
if the or combination over all vertices yields true.

Further to the definition above, we realise some simple policies which
can be summarised as conservative refinement:

1.  All refinement and erase instructions are "at least" instructions.
     will match them, but it might always decide to make the mesh
    slighlty finer. Notably, it might ignore erase instructions.

2.   adds or removes at most one grid level per mesh traversal.

's dynamic AMR is realiesd via `GridControlEvent`s. They mark a certain
area within the domain and clarify which resolution you would like to
have within this area. Each event is also marked with a refine or erase
marker, i.e. you can specify whether you'd expect  to refine towards
this resolution or erase mesh parts up to a certain level of mesh
detail.

 will label a vertex as refine, if any of its adjacent cells overlaps
with a refinement event.  will label vertices as "to be erased"
(i.e. remove their refined flag), if all of its adjacent cells fit into
an erase area. The code will glue various events together, i.e. if you
have a patchwork of refine events, it will create one large refine
event.

If you want to refine a region, create a refinement event which
coincides with the cell size which you want to refine. If you want to
erase a grid region, create a similar event, but then blow it up by a
factor of three. Otherwise,  might simply ignore it.

